{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I appeared to Abraham</b>, etc. — <b>as El Shaddai</b>: I made him many promises, and in all of them I said to them, 'I am El Shaddai.' <b>But My name YHWH I did not make known to them</b> — it is not written לֹא הוֹדַעְתִּי ('I did not make known') here, but rather לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי לָהֶם ('I was not known to them'), meaning: I was not known to them through My true attribute upon which My name YHWH is called, faithful to fulfill the words — for I promised them and did not fulfill them. All this is the language of the Rav [Maimonides]. And he intends to explain that the fulfillment of the promise was not yet known. And even though the time had not yet arrived, in any case it was not known to them in the fulfillment of His promise. Yet despite all this, the formulation is not correct, for it should have said לֹא הוֹדַעְתִּי, or it should say וּשְׁמִי ה׳ לֹא נוֹדַע לָהֶם. But perhaps according to his opinion he reads וּשְׁמִי ה׳ וְלֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי לָהֶם, meaning 'I was not known to them through it.'" "The sage Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that the letter bet in <b>בְּאֵל שַׁדַּי</b> is distributive, and the verse is punctuated as: <b>And I appeared to Abraham</b>, etc., <b>as El Shaddai</b>, and by My name YHWH <b>I was not known to them</b>. The meaning of the verse: For I appeared to the Patriarchs by this name — He who vanquishes the battalions of heaven and performs with them great miracles that do not nullify the natural order of the world — in famine He redeemed them from death and in war from the sword, and He gave them wealth and honor and every good thing. These are like all the promises in the Torah in the blessings and the curses, for no good comes upon a person as reward for a commandment nor evil as punishment for a transgression except through miraculous action, and if a person were left to his nature or his astrological lot, his deeds would add nothing to him nor detract from him. But the reward for the entire Torah and its punishment in this world are all miracles, and they are hidden — it appears to onlookers that it is the natural order of the world, yet upon a person they are truly punishment and reward. And because of this, the Torah elaborates on the promises concerning this world and does not explain the promises of the soul in the world of souls, for these wonders run counter to nature, while the endurance of the soul and its cleaving to Elohim is something fitting to its nature, that it should return to the Elohim who gave it. And I will further explain this (Leviticus 26:11, on 'ונתתי') if HaShem grants me life. Behold, Elohim said to Moses: I appeared to the Patriarchs through the power of My hand, with which I plunder the constellations and aid My chosen ones, but through My name of yod heh — through which everything that exists comes into being — I was not known to them, to create for them new things through alteration of nature. And <b>therefore say to the children of Israel: I am YHWH</b>, and inform them once more of the great name, for through it I act with them wondrously, and they shall know that I am YHWH who does everything.\n\nAnd all the words of R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] in this matter are correct, except that he speaks like a prophet without knowing it. For even according to his own words, Scripture should have said וָאִוָּדַע אֶל אַבְרָהָם, etc., בְּאֵל שַׁדַּי וּבִשְׁמִי ה׳ לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי לָהֶם, or it should say וּבִשְׁמִי ה׳ לֹא נִרְאֵיתִי לָהֶם. But he would correct this, for because the prophecy of the Patriarchs came through visions of the night, Scripture says of them <b>וָאֵרָא</b> ('I appeared'), and because that of Moses was face to face, Scripture says <b>לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי לָהֶם</b> ('I was not known to them') — as I was known to you.\n\nAnd by way of truth, the verse comes according to its plain sense and meaning: it says, 'I am YHWH; I appeared to them in the speculum of El Shaddai,' as in the sense 'in a vision I make Myself known to him' (Numbers 12:6), 'but Me, I am YHWH — <b>I was not known to them</b>,' meaning they did not contemplate in the illuminating speculum (Zohar Va'era 26a) through which they would know Me, as in the sense 'whom YHWH knew face to face' (Deuteronomy 34:10). For the Patriarchs knew YHWH the unique One [המיוחד], but He was not known to them in prophecy. Therefore, when Abraham speaks with HaShem, he mentions the unique Name [המיוחד] together with Adonai, or Adonai alone.\n\nThe matter is this: the Patriarchs had revelation of the Shekhinah granted to them, and discourse with them occurred through the attribute of strict judgment in a weakened mode, and He dealt with them accordingly. But with Moses He conducts Himself and makes Himself known through the attribute of mercy, which is His great name, as in 'who leads to the right hand of Moses the arm of His glory' (Isaiah 63:12), and it is written (Isaiah 63:14), 'so You led Your people, to make for Yourself a name of glory.' Therefore Moses will not mention from now on the name El Shaddai, for the Torah was given through His great name, as it says (Exodus 20:2), 'I am YHWH your Elohim,' and it says (Deuteronomy 4:36), 'From the heavens He made you hear His voice to discipline you, and on the earth He showed you His great fire.' And I have already hinted at the meaning of 'the heavens.' And may the Holy One, blessed be He, open our eyes and show us wonders from His Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {1 "In the passage discussing Ibn Ezra's view, Ramban says 'והנה אמר האלהים למשה' and then paraphrases the divine message; 'אמר' read as narrative ('said'). The phrase 'אם גומר ה׳ עלי' is a common medieval authorial formula meaning 'if HaShem grants me life' to complete a promised future discussion; the word 'גומר' (or 'יגמר') means 'finish/grant completion.' The parenthetical reference '(ויקרא כו יא ד\"ה ונתתי)' cites Leviticus 26:11 with the lemma-head 'ונתתי'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:03:49.770338688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}